.TH CVECTOR 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CVECTOR \- overview of the cvector dynamic array
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A cvec is an array of elements of one type that can change its size, with
the elements contiguous in memory. Access by index is O(1), an append at
the end is amortized O(1), and a sort is O(n log n) and stable, so
elements that are equal keep their relative order.

The capacity starts at 4 elements and never goes below 4. When the vector
is full, the capacity becomes two times larger; when a removal makes the
vector less than a quarter full, the capacity becomes two times smaller.
cvec_reserve(3) and cvec_append_array(3) grow the capacity to the next
power of two at or above the count that they need.

The API has two layers:
  - The type-inferred macros, for example cvec_construct(3),
    cvec_push(3), cvec_at(3) and cvec_sort(3). These macros keep the
    element type with the handle, in companion variables that
    cvec_declare(3), the construct macros and cvec_redeclare(3)
    declare. On a hard error, such as a failed allocation, the macros
    stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
  - The raw functions, for example cvector_create_full(3),
    cvector_push_back(3), cvector_pop_back(3) and cvector_reserve(3),
    which take elements by pointer and give a failure in their return
    value.

The element type can be any type: for example an arithmetic type, a
pointer, a struct, a union, a function pointer type such as
void (*)(int), or an array type such as char[32].
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP OF ELEMENTS"
.nf
The vector keeps a copy of each element. For an element that is a
pointer, the vector keeps only the value of the pointer: it never
follows that address, and it never frees the object at that address.
cvec_destroy(3) and cvec_reset(3) never free the objects that the
elements point to, so the caller must free those objects first.
.fi
.SH "REFERENCES INTO THE VECTOR"
.nf
cvec_data_ptr(3), cvec_at_ptr(3) and the address of a cvec_at(3) element
point into the backing store, and an operation that can make that store
larger or smaller moves it. These operations are:
  - a push
  - an append
  - a reserve
  - a pop
  - a reset
  - the destruction.
After each of these operations, get such a pointer again. You can give
a value that you read from the vector to cvec_push(3), and a pointer
into the vector to cvec_append_array(3): both macros read their source
safely, even when they reallocate the store.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A vector has no internal lock. A caller can share one vector between
threads, but must then serialize each call that changes the vector
against all the other calls on that vector.
.fi
